<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.8.2: Using the Distributive Property to Write Equivalent Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01868</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.8.2: Using the Distributive Property to Write Equivalent Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b5ab0794-daaf-4146-b8ee-89f6e0728f42</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Applying the distributive property to multiply out the factors of, or expand, \(4(x+2)\) gives us \(4x+8\), so we know the two expressions are equivalent. We can use a rectangle with side lengths \((x+2)\) and 4 to illustrate the multiplication.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="Rectangle, divided into 2 smaller rectangles. Top of left rectangle labeled x, top of right rectangle labeled 2. Side length labeled 4. Inside left rectangle, 4 x.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_03_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Draw a diagram to show that \(n(2n+5)\) and \(2n^2+5n\) are equivalent expressions.</para>
            <para id="para-00004">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00006">
                      <media alt="Area model multiplying 2n+5 and n">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_03_02.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">For each expression, use the distributive property to write an equivalent expression. If you get stuck, consider drawing a diagram.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">\(6(\frac{1}{3} n + 2)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00009">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00011">\(2n+12\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00012">\(p(4p+9)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00013">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00015">\(4p^2+9p\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00016">\(5r(r+ \frac{3}{5})\)</para>
            <para id="para-00017">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00018">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00019">\(5r^2+3r\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00020">\((0.5w+7)w\)</para>
            <para id="para-00021">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00023">\(0.5w^2+7w\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>

    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00024">Which expression is equivalent to \(4t(t-\frac{3}{4})\)?</para>
    </note>

    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Distributive Property </title>
      
        <para id="para-00025">
         <emphasis> Distributive Property</emphasis>
      </para>
        
        <para id="para-00026">If \(a\), \(b\), \(c\) are real numbers, then:</para>
        <para id="para-00027">\(a(b+c)=ab+ac\)</para>
        <para id="para-00028">Other forms:</para>
        <para id="para-00029">\(a(b-c)=ab-ac\)</para>
        <para id="para-00030">\((b+c)a=ba+ca\)</para>
      <para id="para-00031">
       <emphasis>Example 1</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00032">Simplify \(3(x + 4)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00033"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute the 3 to both the \(x\) and the 4.
  (<media alt="DIAGRAM SHOWING 3 MULTIPLIED BY X PLUS 4. AN ARROW IS GOING FROM 3 TO X. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM 3 TO 4."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_05_01.jpg"/></media>)</para>
      <para id="para-00034"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the terms.
  \(3(x) + 3(4)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00035"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify.
  \(3x + 12\)</para>
      <para id="para-00036">
       <emphasis>Example 2</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00037">Simplify \(m(7 - 2m)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00038"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute the \(m\) to both the 7 and the \(2m\).
  (<media alt="DIAGRAM SHOWING M TIMES 7 MINUS 2M. AN ARROW IS GOING FROM M TO 7. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM M TO 2M."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_05_02.jpg"/></media>)</para>
      <para id="para-00039"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the terms. Recall \(m \cdot m=m^2\).
  \(m(7) - m(2m)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00040"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify.
  \(7m-2m^2\)
</para>
      <para id="para-00041">
       <emphasis>Example 3</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00042">Simplify \(5x(x-\frac{2}{5})\)</para>
      <para id="para-00043"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute the \(5x\) to both the \(x\) and the \(\frac25\).
  (<media alt="DIAGRAM SHOWING 5X TIMES X MINUS TWO-FIFTHS. AN ARROW IS GOING FROM 5X TO X. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM 5X TO TWO-FIFTHS."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_05_03.jpg"/></media>)</para>
      <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the terms. Recall \(x \cdot x=x^2\).
  \(5x(x)-5x(\frac{2}{5})\)</para>
      <para id="para-00045"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify.
  \(5x^2-2x\)</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
  <title>Try It: Distributive Property</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00046">Simplify \(7y(4y-\frac{3}{7})\)</para>
            <para id="para-00047">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00048">Here is how to simplify the expression using the distributive property:</para>
            <para id="para-00049"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute the \(7y\) to both the \(4y\) and the \(\frac{3}{7}\) .</para>
            <para id="para-00050">
                      <media alt="DIAGRAM SHOWING 7Y TIMES 4Y MINUS THREE-SEVENTHS. AN ARROW IS GOING FROM 7Y TO 4Y. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM 7Y TO THREE-SEVENTHS.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_05_04.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00051"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the terms. Recall \(y \cdot y=y^2\).
    \(7y(4y)-7y(\frac{3}{7})\)</para>
            <para id="para-00052"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify.
    \(28y^2-3y\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
